---

interface Props {
  /**
   * The name of the component being described.
   */
  className?: string

  /**
   * Other tags that may be used with the component while still being accessible
   */
  additionalTagsText?: string

  /**
   *  The text describing the component type specifics, if any
   */
  componentTypeSpecificsText?: string
}

const { className, additionalTagsText, componentTypeSpecificsText } = Astro.props

const compatibleTags = `The <code>.${className}</code> classes are designed to be used with the <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> element, but can be used on <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>${additionalTagsText ? ' ' + additionalTagsText : ''} elements. You should use any semantic element that best fits the context to appropriately convey its purpose to assistive technologies such as screen readers.`
---

<p set:html={compatibleTags} />

<p>Use a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> element for actions or for an element that triggers an in-page functionality (like collapsing content), as it is the most semantically correct and accessible choice.</p>
<p>Use an <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> element only for navigation purpose; it must include an <code>href</code> attribute to be focusable.</p>
<p set:html={componentTypeSpecificsText} />

<p>Generally speaking, you should always ask yourself which tag is appropriate for your specific context. The visual rendering will remain the same anyway:</p>
